Title:
Defender Telemetry (Windows Defender ETW Providers & Events)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post catalogs Windows Defender telemetry exposed via ETW, focusing on events that can be correlated to attacker activity through PIDs, image paths, filenames, and scan reasons. It enumerates multiple Defender-related ETW providers (AMFilter, Antimalware Engine, Engine Instrumentation, Protection, RTP, Scan Interface, Service) and lists their event names plus key arguments. The most actionable section is the Antimalware Engine provider, which includes behavior monitoring events for process creation, module/driver loads, registry operations, file operations, network activity, remote thread creation, and OpenProcess access masks. It also highlights scan lifecycle events (stream scans, UFS scans, cache lookups/adds) and remediation/reporting-style events that can reveal detections and response actions. This is useful for red teamers/pentesters to understand what Defender can observe and for blue teamers to build ETW-based detections and correlations. It’s interesting because it maps low-level Defender internals into concrete, queryable telemetry fields suitable for timeline reconstruction and attribution to specific processes.

Technical Focus:
- ETW providers and event schemas for Microsoft Defender
- Defender Behavior Monitoring event coverage (process/file/registry/network)
- Scan pipeline telemetry (stream scans, UFS scans, scan start/stop)
- Correlation primitives: PID, PPID, image path, command line, file path/USN
- Defender caching/metadata stores (MOAC cache, persisted store, metastore)
- Remediation/detection reporting events (SenseRemediationTask, BM detections)

Use Cases:
- Build ETW-based detections for Defender behavior-monitoring signals (e.g., remote thread creation, suspicious OpenProcess access)
- Correlate offensive actions to Defender scan/detection timelines using PID/image path/file path fields
- Identify which attacker tradecraft is likely to generate Defender telemetry (file writes, registry changes, network connects)
- Validate evasion hypotheses by observing which ETW events still fire during attempted bypasses
- Create lab baselines of Defender ETW noise vs. high-signal events for hunting

Keywords:
Windows Defender, Microsoft Defender, ETW, Event Tracing for Windows, Microsoft-Antimalware-Engine, Microsoft-Antimalware-AMFilter, BehaviorMonitoring, RTP, AMSI, UFS scan, Stream scan, SenseRemediationTask, process creation, remote thread creation, OpenProcess, registry telemetry, file system telemetry, network telemetry, MOAC cache, USN, tamper protection